<!doctype html>
<!--
  The garden, walked.

  Three states on one page, because a walk is one thing and not three: the map
  of ten areas, one area with its plants in it, and one plant with a pad for
  stepping to its neighbours. Moving between them is a fragment change, so the
  back button behaves and a place can be linked to.

  Every area is a theme, and the ten are laid out from `Quotes.Theme.position`
  rather than arranged by hand — see docs/WEBSITE.md §"Walking it". Walking
  right goes from the still and long-span toward the moving and momentary,
  walking down from solitary toward shared.

  **Nothing here is published yet**, and the reason is no longer that there is
  no service. There is one — 20i with a database on it, answering `/api/walk` —
  but what it holds is the Long Walk, which is a different garden: plots of
  forty-eight down either side of a path, drawn at `/walk`. These ten areas are
  laid out by theme and have no table behind them, so this still draws from
  `plots.js`'s stand-in and still says so on the screen in as many words.

  The point of building it before its own service is that the geometry, the pad
  and the keyboard walk can be argued about while they are still cheap to
  change.

  Static, like `/s`: no analytics, no font CDN, no third-party anything.
-->
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1, viewport-fit=cover" />
    <title>Peace Garden</title>
    <meta name="description" content="A garden of plants grown from meetings." />
    <meta name="color-scheme" content="dark light" />
    <!--
      `noindex`, where `/s` deliberately carries no robots directive at all.
      The difference is what each page publishes. `/s` publishes nothing — with
      a seed in it the content is in the fragment, which a crawler never
      receives. The garden publishes plants that people put there, and
      docs/WEBSITE.md settles `noindex` for exactly that. Walkable and indexed
      are different properties; this is the one that was decided.
    -->
    <meta name="robots" content="noindex" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/site.css" />
    <link rel="icon" href="/assets/icon.svg" type="image/svg+xml" />
    <link rel="apple-touch-icon" href="/assets/icon-180.png" />
  </head>

  <body>
    <div class="pool" aria-hidden="true"></div>

    <main class="page" id="page">
      <!--
        The bar. On every page of the site, and the only way between any two of
        them that is not a link somebody happened to put in a paragraph.
        `site.css` §The bar says why it is the masthead with two things added
        rather than a header of its own.
      -->
      <header class="masthead bar">
        <span class="bar__home">
          <img class="mark" src="/assets/icon.svg" alt="" width="44" height="44" />
          <span class="wordmark" id="wordmark">Peace Garden</span>
        </span>
        <!--
          The ways on from here, as glyphs rather than words, so they read the
          same in every language: the garden (the app's own Garden mark,
          `GardenGlyph` in Chrome.swift), what the names mean (an open book,
          the app's `MeaningsGlyph`), and the language. Each glyph's words
          are its name and tooltip, written from the catalogue by the page's
          script. See site.css §The bar's glyphs.
        -->
        <span class="bar__ways">
          <a class="bar-glyph" href="/garden" data-s-label="gardenTitle"><svg class="bar-glyph__mark" viewBox="0 0 15 15" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M 4.674 4.059 C 3.796 1.407, 6.716 1.407, 5.838 4.059 C 8.089 2.405, 8.991 5.181, 6.198 5.166 C 8.467 6.795, 6.105 8.512, 5.256 5.850 C 4.407 8.512, 2.045 6.795, 4.314 5.166 C 1.521 5.181, 2.423 2.405, 4.674 4.059 Z M 5.784 14.100 C 5.652 10.932, 5.124 9.612, 5.256 7.500 M 10.140 14.100 C 9.678 10.932, 9.150 8.556, 8.622 6.180 M 10.140 14.100 C 10.338 10.404, 10.470 6.972, 10.338 3.540 M 10.140 14.100 C 10.800 10.932, 11.658 8.820, 12.318 6.972 M 4.200 14.100 L 11.328 14.100" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke" /></svg></a>
          <a class="bar-glyph" href="/meanings" data-s-label="meaningsTitle"><svg class="bar-glyph__mark" viewBox="0 0 20 20" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M10 5.2C8.2 3.9 5.4 3.5 2.75 3.9V15.4C5.4 15 8.2 15.4 10 16.7C11.8 15.4 14.6 15 17.25 15.4V3.9C14.6 3.5 11.8 3.9 10 5.2ZM10 5.2V16.7" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke" /></svg></a>
          <label class="bar-language">
            <span class="visually-hidden" id="language-label"></span>
            <svg class="bar-language__mark" viewBox="0 0 20 20" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M3 10a7 7 0 1 0 14 0a7 7 0 1 0 -14 0M10 3C7.4 5.3 7.4 14.7 10 17M10 3C12.6 5.3 12.6 14.7 10 17M3 10H17M4.2 6.2H15.8M4.2 13.8H15.8" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke" /></svg>
            <span class="chooser__field">
              <select id="language" class="chooser__select"></select>
              <svg class="chooser__mark" viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M4 6.5 L8 10.5 L12 6.5" /></svg>
            </span>
          </label>
        </span>
      </header>

      <!--
        Said plainly, and not in small print.

        `lang` and `dir` are on it because the sentence is English and the page
        around it may not be. Under Arabic the document runs right to left, and
        an unmarked English sentence inside it is reordered by the bidi
        algorithm at its punctuation: *the garden is not open* / *.yet*, with
        the full stop at the head of the next line. Marking the element is the
        fix rather than a wrapper or an override character, because the fact
        being recorded is that this run is English.

        **Reworded 20 September**, because it had stopped being true. It said
      *every plant here is invented, the garden is not open yet*, on a page
      whose travel area now sends a visitor to a Long Walk that is planted and
      open. A notice that is false about one tenth of what it covers is worse
      than none: it tells somebody who has just come back from `/walk` that
      what they saw was made up.

      It is English because it is temporary — it goes when the garden opens —
        and docs/LANGUAGES.md is where the argument about which of the
        site's own prose gets commissioned is being had. If it is still here
        when that is settled, it belongs in the catalogue and these two
        attributes go.
      -->
      <p class="notice body" id="invented" lang="en" dir="ltr" hidden>
        The plants drawn here stand in for the areas not open yet. Choosing an
        open area goes to its own page, which grows the real plants.
      </p>

      <!-- 1. The map: ten areas, five across and two down. -->
      <section class="garden-map" id="map" aria-label="The garden">
        <div class="garden-grid" id="map-grid"></div>
      </section>

      <!-- 2. One area, and what stands in it. -->
      <section class="garden-area" id="area" hidden>
        <h1 class="heading" id="area-name"></h1>
        <div class="area-plot" id="area-plot" role="group"></div>
        <!--
          Only the areas not open yet are drawn here. Choosing an open one goes
          straight to its own page, which grows its real plants — `walk.js`
          `goTo` — so there is no longer a *Walk this area* link under the plot
          to get there by.
        -->
      </section>

      <!-- 3. One plant, and the four ways out of it. -->
      <section class="garden-plant" id="plant" hidden>
        <p class="plant-name" id="plant-name"></p>
        <p class="label" id="plant-where"></p>

        <!--
          The line under the plant, drawn from the reader's own bank. Change the
          chooser and it says something else — not a translation, because no bank
          is a translation of another. docs/WEBSITE.md §"The garden, walked in
          one language at a time".
        -->
        <blockquote class="passage" id="passage" hidden>
          <p id="passage-text"></p>
          <footer class="label" id="passage-source"></footer>
        </blockquote>
        <div class="pad" id="pad" role="group">
          <button class="pad-key" type="button" data-go="up" aria-label="Up">↑</button>
          <button class="pad-key" type="button" data-go="left" aria-label="Left">←</button>
          <button class="pad-key" type="button" data-go="right" aria-label="Right">→</button>
          <button class="pad-key" type="button" data-go="down" aria-label="Down">↓</button>
        </div>
      </section>
      <!--
        The one control on this page that needs a word of its own. `g` is
        labelled by the wordmark above, which is a proper noun and travels; the
        four directions are labelled by the pad. This is the exception, and it
        earns a catalogue string rather than a hardcoded label in the script.
      -->
      <p class="garden-foot">
        <button class="quiet-button" type="button" id="random" data-s="random"></button>
        <!--
          The key to the map: what brings a plant to each area, and what its
          name says. Beside the one other thing at the foot of the garden, and
          in the same capsule, because both are ways of looking at the whole
          of it rather than at one place in it.
        -->
        <a class="quiet-button" href="/meanings" data-s="meaningsTitle"></a>
      </p>

      <!--
        The foot: one hairline and the privacy notice. The language it used to
        hold went up into the bar, beside the other ways on from a page.
      -->
      <footer class="foot bar-foot">
        <a class="bar-foot__link" href="/privacy" data-s="privacyTitle"></a>
      </footer>
    </main>

    <script type="module" src="/assets/js/walk.js"></script>
  </body>
</html>
